.TH HSH 1 "August 2026" "1.0" "Holberton School Simple Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command line interpreter. It reads commands from
either an interactive terminal or a non-interactive stream (such as
a pipe or a file redirection), executes them, and repeats this
process until it receives an end-of-file condition or the
.B exit
built-in is invoked.
.PP
When
.B hsh
is run from a terminal, it displays a prompt
.RB ( "$ " )
and waits for the user to type a command. When it is not connected
to a terminal (for example when its standard input comes from a
pipe), no prompt is displayed.
.PP
A command line is read until a newline character is encountered. It
is then split into words, and the first word is treated as the name
of the command to execute; the remaining words are passed to that
command as arguments.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
If the command name contains a
.B /
character, it is treated as a relative or absolute path, and
.B hsh
attempts to execute it directly at that location.
.PP
Otherwise,
.B hsh
searches for an executable file with that name in each of the
directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable, in the order they appear, separated by
.BR : .
The first matching executable found is used. If no matching
executable is found in any directory of
.BR PATH ,
.B hsh
prints an error message of the form:
.PP
.RS
.I program_name: line_number: command: not found
.RE
.PP
to the standard error stream, and continues to the next command with
an exit status of 127. In this case, no child process is created.
.SH BUILTIN COMMANDS
.B hsh
implements the following commands internally, without searching
.BR PATH :
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell. The exit status is that of the last command
executed before
.B exit
was called, or 0 if no command has been executed yet.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment, one variable per line, in the
format
.IR NAME=value .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
returns the exit status of the last command it executed. If the
command was not found,
.B hsh
uses the conventional shell exit status of 127. If a command
terminates due to a signal, or if
.B hsh
itself encounters a fatal internal error (such as a failed
.BR fork (2)),
other non-zero values may be returned.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories that
.B hsh
searches, in order, to locate an executable file when the command
name does not contain a
.B /
character.
.SH END-OF-FILE HANDLING
When
.B hsh
encounters an end-of-file condition on its standard input
(typically triggered by pressing
.B Ctrl-D
in interactive mode, or by the end of a piped input), it exits
cleanly, using the exit status of the last command executed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Interactive use:
.RS
.nf
$ ./hsh
$ /bin/ls
file1  file2
$ ls
file1  file2
$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive use:
.RS
.nf
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
file1  file2
.fi
.RE
.SH BUGS
.B hsh
does not support command chaining
.RB ( ; ", " && ", " || ),
pipes
.RB ( | ),
I/O redirection
.RB ( < ", " > ),
comments
.RB ( # ),
variable substitution, quoting, or other shell metacharacters. It
handles only single, simple command lines.
